    "lawCode": "Amd §7-109, Gen Ob L",
    "lawSection": "General Obligations Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:A2500\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the general obligations law, in relation\nto money deposited as security in certain cases and enforcement of trust\nprovisions by attorney general\n\nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill would clarify the Attorney General's authority to take proof\nand issue subpoenas, and would further authorize the Attorney General to\nobtain costs, in connection with an action or a proceeding to compel\ncompliance with the General Obligations Law regarding the collection of\nrent security deposits.\n\nSUMMARY:\n\nThe bill amends General Obligations Law, õ7-109, by adding language\nwhich: a) gives the Attorney General explicit authority to take proof,\nmake a determination of the relevant facts and issue subpoenas in\naccordance with the CPLR in connection with the proposed institution of\nan action or proceeding to compel compliance with the provisions of the\nGeneral Obligations Law regarding residential rent security deposits;\nand b) authorizes, in the court's discretion, an allowance for costs not\nexceeding $2,000 against each respondent found in an action or proceed-\ning brought by the Attorney General to compel compliance with those\nprovisions of the General Obligations Law, to have committed a\nviolation.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nõ7-109 of the General Obligations Law was enacted in 1973 (originally as\nõ7-107) to grant to the Attorney General specific authority to bring an\naction or proceeding against any person, association or corporation that\nhas violated or is violating any of the provisions of Title 1 of Article\n7 of the General Obligations Law which protects a tenant's rent security\ndeposit and requires a landlord to place said deposit in an interest\nbearing account.\nThe enactment of section 7-109 has enabled the Attorney General to\ncombat violations of the rent security deposits law.  For the period\nJanuary 1, 1988 through December 14, 1992, a total of 3,701 rent securi-\nty complaints were received by the Department of Law.  While the bulk of\nthese cases were resolved through mediation, the Attorney General  found\nit necessary on some occasions to institute legal proceedings to protect\nthe rights of tenants who gave security deposits to their landlords.\nThe handling of these cases requires a significant expenditure of\nresources and time in order to ensure that tenants' rent security depos-\nits are protected.\nThe Attorney General was given important, increased enforcement obli-\ngations respecting tenant security deposits by Chapter 917 of the Laws\nof 1984. Two additional sections, 7-107 and 7-108, were added to the\nGeneral Obligations Law by this chapter to ensure the protection of\ntenants' security deposits upon the conveyance of a residential build-\n\ning. These sections cast liability, with appropriate limits, upon the\nbuyers of residential buildings.\nExisting case law holds that the Attorney General's standing to bring an\naction or proceeding to compel compliance with section 7-103 is derived\nsolely from section 7-109, and that he may not sue for refunds of rent\nsecurity deposits under Executive Law, õ63(12).\nFurthermore, since CPLR 8303 authorizes an allowance for costs in\nactions or proceedings brought under Executive Law, õ63(12), and not in\nactions or proceedings brought under General Obligations Law, õ7-109,\nsome courts have held that the Attorney General is not permitted to seek\ncosts in rent security cases.\nThere is no apparent reason to deny the Attorney General a discretionary\naward of costs in rent security cases while permitting such an award in\nall other consumer frauds cases.  No legislative history exists to indi-\ncate any desire to exclude such a costs award.  This bill would correct\nthe problem.\nThis bill also makes it clear that the A.G. has authority under General\nObligations Law õ7-109, to take proof and to issue subpoenas in prepara-\ntion for the institution of an action or proceeding to compel compliance\nwith the law.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis bill was first introduced in 1984 and passed the Assembly that year\n(A. 10013).  It also passed the Assembly in 1985 - A.5756, 1986 -\nA.5756-A,  1987 - A.5970-A, and in 1990 - A.6338, 1991 (S.6464/A.8557)\n1995-96 - A.7639 1997-98 - A.5387-B/S.6134-A\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS: None.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE: Immediately, and shall apply only to actions or\nproceedings commenced on or after its effective date.\n\n",
